    sum: "Framed calligraphic descriptions of the Prophet's appearance, hung in homes as devotional 'portraits without image'.",
    desc: "A hilye renders the textual descriptions (shama'il) of Muhammad's looks and character in tiers of script: a head cartouche with the basmala, a central roundel (göbek) with the description text, crescent side cartouches, and a base prayer — all within gold marbled borders. Because figural portraits were avoided, the hilye became the devotional portrait-substitute hung over doors and in living rooms. Masters like Hafiz Osman standardized the composition, marrying thuluth, naskh and tughra on one page. It is calligraphy functioning as icon, amulet and interior design at once.",
