    desc: "Each Ottoman sultan had a personal tughra drawn by the nishanci's office: his name and titles braided into a mark of vertical shafts, paired loops (beyzes) to the left, and flowing tassels to the right, drawn in ink, gold or carved on seals and coinage. Legibility surrendered to majesty — it functioned like a state logo on firmans, gates, cannon and stamps. The form demonstrates calligraphy pushed to pure emblem, and modern Turkish graphic designers still quote it for institutional identity.",
