    p: "1480-1620s peak", era: "Ottoman classical",
    sum: "Ottoman ceramic ware and wall tile in brilliant underglaze cobalt, turquoise and bole red on a fritware body.",
    desc: "İznik kilns answered court demand with dishes, lamps and — above all — wall tile programs for imperial mosques: big tulips, carnations, hyacinths and serrated saz leaves in cobalt, turquoise, emerald and the famous raised bole red under a clear glaze. The body was white fritware (ground quartz paste) giving colors unmatched clarity. Masterpieces clad the Süleymaniye and Rüstem Pasha mosques; the 'golden horn' style scattered faint red speckles over deep blue. Decline came with the 17th century, but the vocabulary still defines 'Islamic tile' in the popular eye.",
