    sum: "Interlaced polygon-and-star line networks built from five template shapes, generating infinite tessellation.",
    desc: "Girih is the geometric strapwork system of Islamic ornament: draftsmen composed surfaces from a set of five tiles — decagon, pentagon, hexagon, bowtie and rhombus — whose edges carry strap lines that continue across joints, weaving stars and polygons into all-over webs. Found in brick, tile, wood and stone from Nishapur to the Alhambra, later patterns achieve quasi-periodic ('Penrose-like') arrangements at Darb-i Imam. It reads as mathematical lace: no figures, pure relation. Modern recreation runs from laser-cut screens to museum façades.",
