    desc: "Anatolian rugs span Seljuk court carpets, Ushak star-and-medallion weaves that filled European Renaissance paintings, and village pieces — Yörük, Konya, Karapınar — with angular animal and mihrab fields woven from highland wool and glowing madder and indigo dyes. Symmetric Giordes knotting gives a firm, sturdy pile; borders pile motif upon motif in nested bands. Holbein and Lotto paintings canonized their patterns in Europe centuries before 'Ottoman carpet' was a term. Regional variants remain living loom traditions today.",
